#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192387 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7813df9e-9641-58a3-8537-e672e5fd079c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Intercept Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "c0233eba-1101-5c9f-8603-bbfdabb3923f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10095" :question/problem "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7 x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_1,y_1), \\: \\ldots, \\: (x_{8},y_{8})$ of $n=8$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=2.4$, the mean square error of the model is $1.25$, and $S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 = 42$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $6$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is given by $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg)}$*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7x\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0}_{\\:\\: \\textrm{point} \\ \\textrm{estimate}} \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$, given by\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}$\nwhere we have:$\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n$\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.$S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n$\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7x$, our point estimate is\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 1.6$.\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.90=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05$.\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05$. As a result,\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943$.\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.25 \\cdot \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{8} + \\dfrac{2.4^2}{42} \\bigg)} \\approx 1.1$\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = 1.6 \\pm 1.1$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192388 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bbee7f0-8803-54e8-874c-e570323ea33a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "bac24078-f1fb-56ae-ac2b-baa86b3a75ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-10096" :question/problem "| **Hours ($x$)** | **$4$** | **$4.5$** | **$5.5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Grades ($y$)** | $62$ | $65$ | $77$ | $81$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours each of four students spent studying for a biology exam and the grades they obtained on the exam. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x$.\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=5$, and $\\textrm{MSE}=1.375$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is*\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg)}$." :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x= 21.3 + 10x\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\beta_0 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$, given by\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}$\nwhere we have:$\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n$\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.$S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n$\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2$\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=21.3 + 10x$, our point estimate is\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 21.3$.\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05$.\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05$. As a result,\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{2,0.005} = 2.920$.Computing $S_{xx}$, we get\n$∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}|= (4 - 5)^{2} + (4.5 - 5)^{2} + (5.5 - 5)^{2} + (6 - 5)^{2}; = 1 + 0.25 + 0.25 + 1; = 2.5$. Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = 2.920 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.375\\cdot \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{4} + \\dfrac{5^2}{2.5} \\bigg)} \\approx 11.0$\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = 21.3 \\pm 11.0$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7813df9e-9641-58a3-8537-e672e5fd079c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Intercept Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c65fed1-27c9-5cfc-9048-2d116fad5f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5 ± 2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189761} {:answer/id #uuid "b7842007-8121-5dd4-a02c-c92f2536f71b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 ± 1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189762} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1201b7-f41e-5d52-a92b-aa861df718df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5 ± 3.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189763} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a87739-c0db-5853-84a1-48a7c08ba98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5 ± 1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189764} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebecaf8-b15e-5a58-abfe-4e33b137f1b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 ± 2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189765}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2f9df6ed-4f2d-5912-b30b-a8d79df169ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189760}] :question/id #uuid "68d56eaf-9e11-54bc-9fcb-5131b64bc995" :question/math-academy-id "q-143256" :question/problem "Let $y^(^) = β^(^)_{0} + β^(^)_{1}x = 3.5 + 5x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_{1}, y_{1}), …, (x_{20}, y_{20})$ of $n = 20$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $x^(―) = 2$, the mean square error of the model is $8$, and $S_{xx} = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2} = 24$, find a $95%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.101) = 0.025$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $18$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $β^(^)_{0}$ is given by $SE[β^(^)_{0}] = \\sqrt{MSE \\cdot (\\frac{1}{n} + (x^(―)^{2})/(S_{xx}))}$*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
